# 🧰 6. SFC and DISM

## System File Checker (SFC)

SFC checks protected Windows system files and attempts to repair corrupted files.

Command:

```cmd
sfc /scannow
```

Purpose:

- Scan protected Windows system files.
- Detect certain types of system-file corruption.
- Attempt repairs using available repair files.

## DISM

DISM stands for Deployment Image Servicing and Management.

It can repair the Windows component store, which supplies files and resources used for Windows servicing and system repair.

Command:

```cmd
DISM /Online /Cleanup-Image /RestoreHealth
```

Purpose:

- Check and repair the Windows component store.
- Help restore the repair source needed by Windows system-file repair.

## Difference Between SFC and DISM

| SFC | DISM |
|---|---|
| Checks protected Windows system files | Repairs the Windows component store |
| Attempts to repair corrupted system files | Restores the component store when repair is needed |
| Command: `sfc /scannow` | Command: `DISM /Online /Cleanup-Image /RestoreHealth` |

## Common Repair Sequence

When system-file corruption is suspected and repair is appropriate:

```cmd
DISM /Online /Cleanup-Image /RestoreHealth
sfc /scannow
```

Run these commands in an elevated terminal when required and authorized. DISM may need access to Windows Update or an approved repair source.

These commands are not a universal solution for all performance issues.

